# What does NOT get uploaded by `npx vercel deploy --prod`.
#
# That command uploads the local directory, not a commit — so without this file, what ships is
# whatever happens to be sitting in the working tree. This repo's working tree holds a Google
# service account private key (`keys/mantle-sa.json`) and a `.env` with every live credential, both
# gitignored and therefore invisible to `git status`. Whether the CLI falls back to `.gitignore`
# when this file is absent is not something the deploy should depend on: state it here instead.
#
# ⚠ EVERY DIRECTORY PATTERN HERE IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING `/`, and `tests/deploy/vercelignore.test.ts`
# fails the build if one is not. This is gitignore syntax: an unanchored `translation/` matches a
# directory of that name AT ANY DEPTH. The first version of this file was unanchored, which quietly
# excluded `src/domain/translation/` and `src/domain/conversion/` — the function then deployed
# "successfully" and every API request died with
# `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND: /var/task/src/adapters/db/createDb`, because the bundler had nothing to
# inline. The static SPA still served a 200, so the deployment looked fine from the outside.
#
# `vercel.json` is uploaded regardless of what this file says — the platform needs it to build.

# --- credentials. The reason this file exists. Unanchored on purpose: an `.env` at any depth is
#     the same mistake wherever it is.
.env
.env.*
/keys/

# --- pipeline data. The database is the record of truth (docs/ko/env.md); `output/` is only the
#     import/export format for `pnpm db:export` and `pnpm db:import`, both of which run locally.
/output/

# --- steering corpus at the REPO ROOT (not `src/domain/conversion`, which the function needs).
#     Read through `FileConversionConfig`/`JsonGlossaryStore`, which `createDeps` builds ONLY under
#     `if (conversionEnabled)` — false on the hosted route set, where `convert-prepare` is a 404
#     (src/app/createDeps.ts:356). The deployment cannot reach these.
/conversion/
/translation/

# --- installed on Vercel from the lockfile; uploading them wastes the whole upload. Unanchored on
#     purpose: a nested `node_modules/` is just as pointless to ship.
node_modules/

# --- build artefacts. `buildCommand: pnpm build:web` regenerates `web/dist` on Vercel; shipping a
#     local copy would let a stale bundle win over the one the platform just built.
/web/dist/
/dist/
/.vercel/

# --- not part of the running deployment ---
/tests/
/web/tests/
/docs/
/design/
/.github/
/.playwright-mcp/
/.claude/
/.agents/
/.superpowers/
/vitest.config.ts
/vitest.probe.config.ts
*.md
*.xlsx
